WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.222
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book two.

2
00:00:05.522 --> 00:00:08.990
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.290 --> 00:00:16.203
Unit twenty-eight: Philosophy of Happiness. Today you will relate work and wellbeing using comparative correlatives.

4
00:00:16.503 --> 00:00:19.399
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.333 --> 00:00:23.671
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.517 --> 00:00:25.587
Happiness.

7
00:00:26.583 --> 00:00:29.477
Happiness means a state of wellbeing and contentment.

8
00:00:30.083 --> 00:00:32.605
Happiness is often confused with pleasure.

9
00:00:33.217 --> 00:00:35.962
Your turn. Say: Happiness.

10
00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:39.882
Happiness.

11
00:00:40.617 --> 00:00:41.583
Wellbeing.

12
00:00:42.583 --> 00:00:47.056
Wellbeing means the overall condition of being healthy, comfortable and fulfilled.

13
00:00:47.650 --> 00:00:50.764
Wellbeing includes health, relationships and purpose.

14
00:00:51.350 --> 00:00:53.677
Your turn. Say: Wellbeing.

15
00:00:56.533 --> 00:00:57.453
Wellbeing.

16
00:00:58.217 --> 00:00:59.067
Virtue.

17
00:01:00.083 --> 00:01:03.023
Virtue means a trait of character that is morally good.

18
00:01:03.617 --> 00:01:06.731
Aristotle considered virtue essential to happiness.

19
00:01:07.317 --> 00:01:09.795
Your turn. Say: Virtue.

20
00:01:12.633 --> 00:01:13.414
Virtue.

21
00:01:14.150 --> 00:01:15.012
Pleasure.

22
00:01:16.017 --> 00:01:18.945
Pleasure means a feeling of enjoyment or satisfaction.

23
00:01:19.550 --> 00:01:22.490
Epicurus valued simple pleasures over luxury.

24
00:01:23.083 --> 00:01:25.515
Your turn. Say: Pleasure.

25
00:01:28.367 --> 00:01:29.275
Pleasure.

26
00:01:30.017 --> 00:01:30.751
Meaning.

27
00:01:31.750 --> 00:01:35.027
Meaning means a sense of purpose and significance in life.

28
00:01:35.617 --> 00:01:38.290
Research links meaning to lasting satisfaction.

29
00:01:38.883 --> 00:01:40.920
Your turn. Say: Meaning.

30
00:01:43.767 --> 00:01:44.489
Meaning.

31
00:01:45.250 --> 00:01:46.228
Flourishing.

32
00:01:47.217 --> 00:01:51.817
Flourishing means living well and realising one's potential, the Greek eudaimonia.

33
00:01:52.417 --> 00:01:54.927
Flourishing is more than feeling good for a moment.

34
00:01:55.517 --> 00:01:58.030
Your turn. Say: Flourishing.

35
00:02:00.867 --> 00:02:01.868
Flourishing.

36
00:02:02.617 --> 00:02:03.560
Contentment.

37
00:02:04.550 --> 00:02:07.920
Contentment means a peaceful satisfaction with what one has.

38
00:02:08.517 --> 00:02:11.143
The Stoics prized contentment over ambition.

39
00:02:11.750 --> 00:02:14.251
Your turn. Say: Contentment.

40
00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:18.124
Contentment.

41
00:02:18.883 --> 00:02:19.745
Good life.

42
00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:23.841
Good life means a life that is worthwhile and well lived.

43
00:02:24.450 --> 00:02:27.053
Philosophers debate what constitutes the good life.

44
00:02:27.650 --> 00:02:30.360
Your turn. Say: Good life.

45
00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:34.189
Good life.

46
00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:38.361
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:39.217 --> 00:02:43.715
Visal, I earn more every year, but I feel no happier. Why?

48
00:02:44.817 --> 00:02:52.368
Because wellbeing depends on meaning more than on income. The more meaningful our work, the happier we become.

49
00:02:53.483 --> 00:02:54.914
So money doesn't matter?

50
00:02:56.017 --> 00:03:04.764
It matters up to a point. The greater our security, the less we worry, yet the more we chase luxury, the less we are satisfied.

51
00:03:05.850 --> 00:03:07.455
How do I find meaning, then?

52
00:03:08.550 --> 00:03:16.900
The clearer your values, the easier your choices become, and the more you contribute to others, the richer your life feels.

53
00:03:18.067 --> 00:03:19.521
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:20.383 --> 00:03:23.300
Grammar focus: Comparative correlatives.

55
00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:24.729
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:25.417 --> 00:03:28.148
The more meaningful our work, the happier we become.

57
00:03:29.050 --> 00:03:31.526
The greater our security, the less we worry.

58
00:03:32.417 --> 00:03:35.542
The clearer your values, the easier your choices become.

59
00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:46.548
The + comparative, the + comparative shows parallel change: The more X, the more Y. Both parts need 'the'; the verb 'be' can be dropped: The sooner, the better.

60
00:03:47.283 --> 00:03:51.468
Be careful! Don't say: More meaningful our work, more happy we are.

61
00:03:53.433 --> 00:03:57.128
Say: The more meaningful our work, the happier we are.

62
00:03:57.428 --> 00:03:59.903
the + comparative, the + comparative.

63
00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:03.387
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:05.793
What Makes a Life Good?.

65
00:04:06.093 --> 00:04:13.666
Since antiquity, philosophers have asked what it means to live well, and their answers fall broadly into pleasure, virtue and meaning.

66
00:04:14.667 --> 00:04:21.194
Epicurus argued that the simpler our desires, the easier they are to satisfy, and that tranquillity is the highest pleasure.

67
00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:29.018
Aristotle replied that happiness is flourishing, an activity of the soul in accordance with virtue over a whole lifetime.

68
00:04:30.033 --> 00:04:39.591
Modern research broadly agrees that the stronger our relationships and the more meaningful our work, the higher our reported wellbeing, whereas wealth beyond a certain level adds little.

69
00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:45.676
Contentment, then, appears less a prize to be captured than a by-product of living with purpose.

70
00:04:46.767 --> 00:04:51.302
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:52.150 --> 00:04:55.102
The more meaningful our work, blank happier we become.

72
00:04:55.402 --> 00:04:58.173
A, the. B, more. C, a.

73
00:05:01.733 --> 00:05:03.152
The answer is A: the.

74
00:05:04.317 --> 00:05:07.268
Living well and realising one's potential is blank.

75
00:05:07.568 --> 00:05:11.442
A, pleasure. B, flourishing. C, contentment only.

76
00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:16.860
The answer is B: flourishing.

77
00:05:18.017 --> 00:05:20.330
Aristotle linked happiness to blank.

78
00:05:20.630 --> 00:05:23.807
A, virtue. B, luxury. C, chance.

79
00:05:27.367 --> 00:05:29.297
The answer is A: virtue.

80
00:05:30.450 --> 00:05:32.217
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:32.517 --> 00:05:40.908
A, The more we study, the wiser we become.. B, More we study, wiser we become.. C, The more we study, more wise we become..

82
00:05:44.467 --> 00:05:47.674
The answer is A: The more we study, the wiser we become..

83
00:05:48.817 --> 00:05:51.513
According to the reading, wealth beyond a certain level…

84
00:05:51.813 --> 00:05:55.733
A, adds little. B, guarantees joy. C, ends meaning.

85
00:05:59.267 --> 00:06:01.197
The answer is A: adds little.

86
00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:05.702
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:06.550 --> 00:06:08.410
What do you think makes a life good?

88
00:06:25.217 --> 00:06:26.763
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:27.063 --> 00:06:53.359
A good life, I think, combines meaningful work, close relationships and a sense of moral integrity. The more we live by our values, the less we depend on approval, and the more stable our wellbeing becomes. Pleasure and comfort matter, but they are not enough alone. A life is good when, looking back, we can recognise that we have contributed something beyond ourselves.

90
00:06:53.659 --> 00:06:55.345
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:56.250 --> 00:07:00.400
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and comparative correlatives.

92
00:07:00.700 --> 00:07:04.676
Today's value is gratitude. Say thank you for your home and family.

93
00:07:04.976 --> 00:07:07.593
See you in Unit twenty-nine. Goodbye!

94
00:07:07.893 --> 00:07:10.430
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

